    "contentEn": "## 1. Right Mindset: Tarot as a Mirror of the Subconscious\n\nTarot is not a deterministic crystal ball that sentences you to a fixed fate. It functions as an **archetypal mirror reflecting your subconscious mind**. Every card you draw reveals underlying currents, blind spots, and latent potentials so you can make informed, conscious decisions.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Preparation and Grounding Before Drawing Cards\n\nBefore touching the deck, take three deep, slow breaths. Clear away ambient mental noise. Formulate a single, clear question centered on self-empowerment rather than passive prediction.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Provably Fair Shuffling and Drawing with Intention\n\nWhether handling physical cards or utilizing our cryptographic Web Crypto shuffle, focus your mind on your question as you mix the cards. Cut the deck where intuition leads you. True randomness eliminates human bias.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Selecting the Right Spread for Beginner Questions\n\n- **1-Card Draw**: Daily reflection, focal energy, or immediate clarity.\n- **3-Card Spread**: Past / Present / Future, or Mind / Body / Spirit.\n- **Problem & Solution**: Obstacle vs. Empowering Action.\n\n---\n\n## 5. 3-Dimensional Interpretation: Symbols, Elements, Story\n\nObserve each card across three core layers:\n1. **Core Symbolism**: What colors, figures, and landscapes stand out?\n2. **Elemental Suit**: Is the situation dominated by action (Wands), emotion (Cups), intellect (Swords), or physical stability (Pentacles)?\n3. **Narrative Flow**: How do neighboring cards converse with one another?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Common Beginner Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them\n\nAvoid obsessively re-shuffling when an uncomfortable card appears. Accept every card as constructive counsel. Trust your initial intuitive impressions rather than frantically memorizing rote booklet definitions."
